<workout_file>
    <author>Tom Jelen</author>
    <name>Weeks 3-4 - 4x4 @ 50-60 rpm</name>
    <activitySaveName>High Torque: Weeks 3-4 - 4x4 min 80-85% FTP @ 50-60 rpm</activitySaveName>
    <description>Adaptation weeks 3–4. Four 4-minute intervals at 80–85% FTP and 50–60 rpm, with 4 minutes of easy spin between them. Seated throughout. My combination of three coaches' advice: the cadence and intensity follow Neal Henderson's advice for riders just starting torque work (tempo efforts at 75–85% of threshold at 50–60 rpm, Fast Talk podcast ep. 125, 2020, machine transcript); Peter Schep (EF Pro Cycling) starts riders with &quot;60 rpms at 80 to 85% of your FTP&quot; for 15 minutes; the four 4-minute reps with rest as long as the rep come from John Wakefield (That Triathlon Show #465 podcast, machine transcript). The file holds 80%, the bottom of the range. The 4-week adaptation (4×4 at 60–65 rpm in weeks 1–2, then this session in weeks 3–4, one session a week) is my arrangement.</description>
    <sportType>bike</sportType>
    <tags />
    <workout>
        <Warmup Duration="900" PowerLow="0.40" PowerHigh="0.80">
            <textevent timeoffset="5" duration="15" message="Warm up at your normal cadence. No low cadence until the first interval." />
            <textevent timeoffset="30" duration="30" message="For riders starting torque work, coach Neal Henderson suggests tempo efforts of 3-5 minutes at 75-85% of threshold and 50-60 rpm. That's where this session comes from." />
            <textevent timeoffset="90" duration="20" message="All the research behind this program: high-torque.jelen.dk" />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="35" message="This is the second half of the 4-week adaptation: two weeks at 50-60 rpm, the lowest cadence the 8-week build uses." />
            <textevent timeoffset="240" duration="25" message="Same reps and power as the first two weeks, but at 50-60 rpm instead of 60-65. More force on every pedal stroke." />
            <textevent timeoffset="420" duration="30" message="Stay seated for every interval. Coach Neal Henderson's reason: seated riding puts less compression on your joints." />
            <textevent timeoffset="600" duration="25" message="Target cadence: 50-60 rpm. In ERG mode, just drop to this cadence. The trainer raises the resistance to hold the power." />
            <textevent timeoffset="720" duration="20" message="If your knees ache at any point during an interval, stop the interval. Don't push through knee discomfort." />
            <textevent timeoffset="820" duration="20" message="Controlled: steady, below your threshold. Heavy legs are normal." />
        </Warmup>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 1. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Steady, below threshold." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Easy spin at whatever cadence feels natural." />
            <textevent timeoffset="60" duration="25" message="At the same power, 50-60 rpm means roughly 1.5 to 1.8 times the force on each pedal stroke of 90 rpm. That extra force per stroke is the point of torque work." />
            <textevent timeoffset="150" duration="30" message="Coach Neal Henderson: 'the first thing I would do is a little bit of tempo … three to five minute efforts that are more like 75 to 85% of threshold power at say 50 to 60 rpm.'" />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 2. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Smooth pedal strokes." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Spin freely." />
            <textevent timeoffset="40" duration="30" message="Tip: if you can't hold the power at this cadence, lower the workout intensity a few percent rather than grinding. These weeks are the low-stakes place to learn this." />
            <textevent timeoffset="160" message="Two down, two to go. Knees still feeling fine?" />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 3. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Heavy legs are normal." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Three down, one to go." />
            <textevent timeoffset="160" duration="20" message="If your knees are aching, skip the final interval. Protecting the joints is always the right call on torque work." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Final interval. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="20" message="Halfway. Last effort. Keep the cadence low and the pedal stroke smooth." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds. Finish it." />
        </SteadyState>

        <Cooldown Duration="600" PowerLow="0.50" PowerHigh="0.40">
            <textevent timeoffset="30" message="Cool down. Spin easy at normal cadence." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="25" message="Note how your knees feel. Any ache? Then repeat this session next week instead of moving on. If it keeps coming back, stop torque work and see a professional." />
            <textevent timeoffset="270" duration="30" message="Ready for the build? Move on after two of these sessions if your knees were fine, you held the cadence, and you weren't wrecked the next day. Otherwise, repeat this one." />
            <textevent timeoffset="420" duration="25" message="Leave at least a full day before your next torque session. No back-to-back days." />
        </Cooldown>
    </workout>
</workout_file>
